    {
      "block_id": "BL-6137-3.3.05-10",
      "block_type": "plain_explanation",
      "heading": "",
      "text": "Traffic congestion occurs where travel demand and usable capacity conflict, often at peaks or bottlenecks. More vehicles can create queues, longer journeys, emissions and noise. Public transport, land-use coordination, suitable crossings and demand management can address different mechanisms. Adding lanes may temporarily improve capacity but can also attract additional trips; the long-term effect needs evidence rather than an automatic prediction.",
      "claim_ids": [
        "CL-6137-3.3.05-1",
        "CL-6137-3.3.05-2",
        "CL-6137-3.3.05-01-1",
        "CL-6137-3.3.05-01-2",
        "CL-6137-3.3.05-02-1",
